{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n ':yin.k/store|resources|resource' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1,125p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '808,848p'\"","aggregated_output":"11:2. **Recursive Portable Encoding:** Section 1.1 permits host functions and local stream handles in the environment. A recursive encoding over a **disjoint tagged grammar** is required — one a program's own map literals cannot counterfeit — to encode these local references into portable descriptors, together with per-primitive **semantic profiles** (a name recovers a binding, not a meaning) and explicit, total failure modes for un-serializable resources. Cursor aliasing is observable and must survive the round trip.\n13:4. **Ownership Arbitration:** Emitting a continuation does not transfer ownership. Exclusion requires a **grounded authority** — a resource the grantor's boundary actually possesses, named in the value, independent of carriers — plus an **exporting state** that fences the source before publication, and **effect fencing** with authority epochs and stable operation ids, because absence of a lapse record is never evidence of tenure.\n41:plus a reason and resource ids, with no handle and no closure attached. That\n100: :yin.k/store       {…}                        ; §7.6.2  the store slice restored as an isolated store\n108:`:yin.k/cells`, `:yin.k/store`, `:yin.k/scheduler`), *what world*\n467:A parked machine was waiting for something. That something is a resource of\n469:the wait in resource-independent terms so the resumer can re-establish an\n734:host resources behind stream descriptors and named, profiled primitives,\n822::yin.k/store {counter 41\n842:receiver key participates; resource entries are remapped to fresh local\n843:keys (cursor cells per §7.5.3, stream handles per §7.4.3), and non-resource\n976:boundary that possesses the resource** and must reclaim *by acting on what\n985:**The possessed resource is the occurrence's execution ledger**: the set of\n1089:admission resource, never check-then-act. An effect whose incarnation's\n1091:`dao.lease.md`'s \"fencing from the resource\" for durable resources, and it\n1115:from the resource.\" No callback tells a lapsed holder to stop; it stops at\n1143:loss breaks exclusive custody irreparably — the ledger was the resource —\n1147:duties*: a judge whose durability matches the resource).\n1169:end the prior tenure). Its inventory of possessed resources is the set of\n1187:   refusal; compute `:yin.k/requires` and `:yin.k/store` by the fixed point\n   808\tundeclared footprint cannot be closed — the result distinguishes\n   809\t`:complete` from `:incomplete` from `:blocked` (§7.6.5); a fixed point that\n   810\tcould not finish is reported as such, not passed off as a satisfied closure.\n   811\t\n   812\t### 7.6.2 The store slice, restored as an isolated store\n   813\t\n   814\tThe store S holds `def` results, stream handles, cursor entries, and the FFI\n   815\tpair (§4.1). None of it is global in the UCF sense; it is the emitter's. A\n   816\tcontinuation carries the **reachable slice**: every store key the fixed\n   817\tpoint of §7.6.1 can name — every free `:var` obligation for which the\n   818\temitter's store has a matching key, every `:store-get`/`:store-put` operand,\n   819\tevery stream/cursor cell — with values encoded by §7.5:\n   820\t\n   821\t```clojure\n   822\t:yin.k/store {counter 41\n   823\t              'log {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}}\n   824\t```\n   825\t\n   826\tThe FFI pair is never in the slice — a program that names the pair's store\n   827\tkeys directly holds handles, and the lift refuses it (§7.5.4). A resumer has\n   828\tits own pair; outstanding calls route per §7.4.3.\n   829\t\n   830\t**There is no merge.** The first draft said, in two places, both that the\n   831\tslice wins on collision and that the slice merges *under* the local store —\n   832\ta contradiction, and beneath it a real defect: resolution is env → store →\n   833\tprimitives (`resolve-var`), so a resumer whose local store happens to bind\n   834\t`+` would change what the migrated program resolves, without any collision\n   835\tat all. Merging in either order is the wrong shape. **The resumed task runs\n   836\tin an isolated execution store**: a fresh store whose contents are exactly\n   837\tthe carried slice, the remapped cells of §7.5.3, and the resumer's FFI pair\n   838\t— nothing else of the resumer's, nothing of any other task's. A name absent\n   839\tfrom the slice resolves through primitives and modules at the resumer\n   840\texactly as it did at the emitter, because there is no third store to\n   841\tintervene; a slice key cannot collide with a receiver task's key because no\n   842\treceiver key participates; resource entries are remapped to fresh local\n   843\tkeys (cursor cells per §7.5.3, stream handles per §7.4.3), and non-resource\n   844\tentries keep their keys verbatim. \"Unreachable store entries do not travel\"\n   845\tsurvives unchanged — anything the program did not observe is not the\n   846\tprogram's state — and nothing else shares state with the resumed task by\n   847\taccident.\n   848\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
