{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '450,530p;550,580p;680,715p;745,770p;812,850p;1180,1230p'\"","aggregated_output":"   450\t  undefined elsewhere. The conformance obligation is `lift ∘ lower = id` on\n   451\t  canonical frames, tested against the reference machine's own frames —\n   452\t  which is possible precisely because the dynamic half rides in the frame.\n   453\t- A request to lift at a pc that is not a safepoint of the segment returns\n   454\t  `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` naming the pc. An engine that wants finer\n   455\t  interruption granularity must lower the program to a segment that\n   456\t  *contains* a `:park` where it wants one: that is a different instruction\n   457\t  record, a different address, and a different profile. An engine may not\n   458\t  invent safepoints a record does not have, and two engines that disagree\n   459\t  about the safepoint set of one address are not both conforming.\n   460\t\n   461\tSafepoints are datoms, so a query can ask \"where can this program be\n   462\tmigrated?\" without running it, and a debugger's breakpoint set is a subset of\n   463\tthe safepoint set by construction.\n   464\t\n   465\t### 7.4.3 The pending wait travels as data — complete, and resumable elsewhere\n   466\t\n   467\tA parked machine was waiting for something. That something is a resource of\n   468\tthe emitter's host, and the resumer cannot poll it. `:yin.k/pending` records\n   469\tthe wait in resource-independent terms so the resumer can re-establish an\n   470\t*equivalent* wait, or decide it cannot. The first draft carried sketches;\n   471\tthe set here is exhaustive over §7.4.1's reasons, and each variant carries\n   472\teverything a *foreign* resumer needs — including the state the first draft\n   473\tleft implicit on the emitter's heap:\n   474\t\n   475\t```clojure\n   476\t;; blocked read (:stream-next)\n   477\t{:yin.k/reason   :next\n   478\t :yin.k/cell     :yin.k/c-17}          ; the cursor CELL id, §7.5.3 — not a bare position\n   479\t\n   480\t;; blocked write (:stream-put, or an effectful call's :stream/put)\n   481\t{:yin.k/reason   :put\n   482\t :yin.k/stream   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; the target\n   483\t :yin.k/value    <encoded>}            ; the retained value — a retry appends THIS, and\n   484\t                                       ; without it the wait is not re-establishable\n   485\t\n   486\t;; FFI call, sent (:ffi-call → ok)\n   487\t{:yin.k/reason     :ffi\n   488\t :yin.k/call-id    :call-7             ; the :dao.stream.apply/id correlation\n   489\t :yin.k/op         :op/add\n   490\t :yin.k/request    {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the request went\n   491\t :yin.k/response   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the answer lands\n   492\t :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23         ; the response cursor cell, §7.5.3, at its KEPT position}\n   493\t\n   494\t;; FFI call, retained (:ffi-call → full): the request is still in hand\n   495\t{:yin.k/reason     :ffi-request\n   496\t :yin.k/call-id    :call-7\n   497\t :yin.k/request-op :op/add\n   498\t :yin.k/request-args [<encoded> …]     ; enough to rebuild the envelope verbatim\n   499\t :yin.k/request    {identity/descriptor as above}\n   500\t :yin.k/response   {identity/descriptor as above}\n   501\t :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23}        ; response cell at its kept position\n   502\t```\n   503\t\n   504\tThree rules make these resumable somewhere other than where they were minted:\n   505\t\n   506\t- **The response position is a kept cursor, never an anchor.** Reattaching\n   507\t  at `:oldest` replays responses that belong to other calls; attaching at\n   508\t  `:newest` skips past this call's own. The response cell therefore travels\n   509\t  at its *kept* position, and the whole task's waiters on that cell travel\n   510\t  with it (§7.6.3), so the round-order semantics of `check-wait-set` —\n   511\t  waiters sharing a cell consume successive values — are preserved on the\n   512\t  resumer exactly. A first poll that observes `gap` is the honest outcome\n   513\t  the contract already promises a kept cursor (`dao.stream.md`, *Retention\n   514\t  and Gaps*). This requires the response transport to accept a kept cursor\n   515\t  from another host, which `dao.stream.md` leaves transport-owned and TBD;\n   516\t  UCF therefore declares, per stream, a **portable cursor profile** in\n   517\t  `:yin.k/requires` (`:yin.k/cursor-profiles`), and a transport that does\n   518\t  not declare one cannot be a pending-response endpoint — the lift refuses\n   519\t  it as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before the value is ever minted.\n   520\t- **Outstanding calls route to the emitter's pair; the resumer's pair is its\n   521\t  own.** The reference machine restores a `:request-sent` entry into a\n   522\t  response wait through its fixed local store keys (`vm/call-out-stream-key`\n   523\t  and friends). A resumer must *not* do that: the outstanding call's\n   524\t  response will arrive on the **emitter's** response stream, because that is\n   525\t  where the request was sent. The resumer re-establishes the wait against\n   526\t  the carried response descriptor and the cell's fresh local key (the cell\n   527\t  id remapped per §7.5.3), while calls the resumed program makes *after*\n   528\t  lowering use the resumer's own pair under its own keys. Pending-call\n   529\t  routing and future-call routing are separate stores of state; conflating\n   530\t  them strands the migrated call exactly as the review described.\n   550\t`{:yin.k/ref addr}` — while passing program map literals through in their\n   551\tnatural shape. That is ambiguous to everyone: §2.5 admits map literals,\n   552\tso a program can *contain* `{:yin.k/primitive '+}`, and a decoder cannot tell\n   553\tan encoder's marker from a program's data. The grammar here is disjoint by\n   554\tconstruction: **every map at an encoded-value position is a UCF marker**, and\n   555\ta program's own maps are always wrapped.\n   556\t\n   557\t| Encoded form | Tag | Contents | Decodes to |\n   558\t|---|---|---|---|\n   559\t| scalars: nil, boolean, number, string, keyword, symbol | — | themselves (never a map; cannot forge a marker) | themselves |\n   560\t| vector, list, set | — | same shape, elements recursively encoded (never a map) | same shape, elements decoded |\n   561\t| literal map | `:yin.k/literal` | `:yin.k/entries [k₁ v₁ k₂ v₂ …]`, keys and values recursively encoded | the map, keys and values decoded |\n   562\t| closure | `:yin.k/closure` | `:yin.k/segment addr :yin.k/entry pc :yin.k/params […] :yin.k/env {encoded}` | `{:type :closure …}` (§2.4) |\n   563\t| reified continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame (§7.4.1), recursively | `{:type :reified-continuation …}` |\n   564\t| parked continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame plus `:yin.k/parked-id` | `{:type :parked-continuation …}` |\n   565\t| primitive | `:yin.k/primitive` | `:yin.k/name sym` | the named function, after profile check (§7.5.2) |\n   566\t| stream reference | `:yin.k/stream` | `:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …` | an attached handle, under a fresh store key |\n   567\t| cursor reference | `:yin.k/cursor-ref` | `:yin.k/cell cell-id` | a store cursor entry for that cell, §7.5.3 |\n   568\t| shared subvalue | `:yin.k/ref` | `:yin.k/id addr` | the table entry at `addr`, §7.5.3 |\n   569\t| anything else | — | — | **refused**: `:yin.k/non-portable` |\n   570\t\n   571\tWhy the wrapping is total rather than an \"escaping rule\" for suspicious\n   572\tshapes: a literal map is wrapped *wherever it occurs and whatever it\n   573\tcontains* — including one that happens to read `{:yin.k/tag …}` — and the\n   574\tentries inside a wrapper are themselves encoded, so a forged marker inside a\n   575\tliteral is re-wrapped one level down and peeled back exactly one level on\n   576\tdecode. No rule asks whether a map \"looks like\" data; no decoder decision\n   577\tdepends on program content. Scalar and collection values pass through only\n   578\tbecause no marker is ever a non-map, which the closed tag table fixes.\n   579\t\n   580\tMap keys are values and are encoded as such — inside `:yin.k/entries`\n   680\t\n   681\t**Cursor cells.** A cursor is *observable state with aliasing*: two distinct\n   682\tcursor refs may sit at the same position and yet be independently\n   683\tadvanceable store entries, while repeated refs to one cursor ref must\n   684\tadvance together — `check-wait-set` advances a shared cursor ref before\n   685\tpolling the next waiter, so waiters on one ref read successive values, and\n   686\twaiters on two refs at the same position read the same next value\n   687\t(`yin.vm.engine`, `check-wait-set`). A position-only encoding erases\n   688\texactly this distinction: two cells at one position would collapse, and\n   689\ttheir independent advancement would be lost. The encoding therefore carries\n   690\t**logical cursor cells** separately from position content:\n   691\t\n   692\t```clojure\n   693\t:yin.k/cells {:yin.k/c-17 {:yin.k/stream   {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}\n   694\t                           :yin.k/position <opaque, exactly as the transport minted it>}}\n   695\t;; values reference cells, never positions:\n   696\t{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/cursor-ref :yin.k/cell :yin.k/c-17}\n   697\t```\n   698\t\n   699\tCell ids are minted by the emitter (stable within one lift; remapped on\n   700\tlowering) and are *not* content-addressed — a cell is a local identity, like\n   701\ta tempid, and deduplicating cells by their contents is precisely the aliasing\n   702\terasure this structure exists to prevent. On lowering, each cell becomes a\n   703\tfresh store cursor entry seeded with its carried position; every\n   704\t`:yin.k/cursor-ref` to it remaps to that entry; two refs to one cell share\n   705\tone entry and one ref per cell keeps its own. The FFI response cell of\n   706\t§7.4.3 is a cell like any other.\n   707\t\n   708\tA stream reference is portable iff its transport produces a descriptor whose\n   709\t`attach!` can succeed somewhere other than the emitter's host. The encoder\n   710\tcannot know that; it encodes the descriptor and lets the resumer's `attach!`\n   711\treport `not-found` as data. What the encoder *can* refuse is a handle whose\n   712\t`descriptor` call fails, which cannot happen under the contract, and a raw\n   713\tin-memory handle admitted by an in-process stream's admission declaration\n   714\t(`dao.stream.md`, *Creation and Attachment*), which it treats as a host\n   715\tobject.\n   745\tvalue. `:yin.k/requires` carries that decision's inputs, and every set in it\n   746\tis **computed by a conservative fixed point**, not hand-written and not\n   747\tguessed from the current environment:\n   748\t\n   749\t```clojure\n   750\t:yin.k/requires\n   751\t{:yin.k/segments        #{:segment/sha256-3f1a… :segment/sha256-9c02…}  ; every address in frame, k, closures, values, parked\n   752\t :yin.k/primitives      {sym → profile, §7.5.2}                        ; every {:yin.k/primitive …} encoded, plus every free :var\n   753\t :yin.k/modules         {my.mod :segment/sha256-…}                     ; module name → manifest address\n   754\t :yin.k/effects         #{:stream/next :stream/put :module/require}    ; effect kinds the segments can raise\n   755\t :yin.k/ffi-ops         #{:op/add}                                     ; :yin.code/ffi-op values present\n   756\t :yin.k/streams         #{<identity> …}                                ; logical-stream identities referenced anywhere\n   757\t :yin.k/cursor-profiles #{:dao.stream/file-v1}                         ; transports that must accept kept cursors, §7.4.3\n   758\t :yin.k/discovery       :complete}                                     ; :complete | :incomplete | :blocked — computed, §7.6.5\n   759\t```\n   760\t\n   761\tThe fixed point starts from the frame, its K frames, the parked records of\n   762\t§7.6.3, the store slice, and the pending wait. Its unit of analysis is a work\n   763\titem `[code-address context]`, not an address: `context` is the finite\n   764\tabstraction of the captured environment with which a closure enters that\n   765\tcode. Code is fetched, validated, and projected once per address, but every\n   766\tnewly discovered address-context pair is analyzed. Two closures over the\n   767\tsame address with different captured environments are therefore distinct\n   768\twork items. A full pass repeats whenever it adds a work item or any dependency\n   769\tfact, and convergence is reached only when a full pass adds neither:\n   770\t\n   812\t### 7.6.2 The store slice, restored as an isolated store\n   813\t\n   814\tThe store S holds `def` results, stream handles, cursor entries, and the FFI\n   815\tpair (§4.1). None of it is global in the UCF sense; it is the emitter's. A\n   816\tcontinuation carries the **reachable slice**: every store key the fixed\n   817\tpoint of §7.6.1 can name — every free `:var` obligation for which the\n   818\temitter's store has a matching key, every `:store-get`/`:store-put` operand,\n   819\tevery stream/cursor cell — with values encoded by §7.5:\n   820\t\n   821\t```clojure\n   822\t:yin.k/store {counter 41\n   823\t              'log {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}}\n   824\t```\n   825\t\n   826\tThe FFI pair is never in the slice — a program that names the pair's store\n   827\tkeys directly holds handles, and the lift refuses it (§7.5.4). A resumer has\n   828\tits own pair; outstanding calls route per §7.4.3.\n   829\t\n   830\t**There is no merge.** The first draft said, in two places, both that the\n   831\tslice wins on collision and that the slice merges *under* the local store —\n   832\ta contradiction, and beneath it a real defect: resolution is env → store →\n   833\tprimitives (`resolve-var`), so a resumer whose local store happens to bind\n   834\t`+` would change what the migrated program resolves, without any collision\n   835\tat all. Merging in either order is the wrong shape. **The resumed task runs\n   836\tin an isolated execution store**: a fresh store whose contents are exactly\n   837\tthe carried slice, the remapped cells of §7.5.3, and the resumer's FFI pair\n   838\t— nothing else of the resumer's, nothing of any other task's. A name absent\n   839\tfrom the slice resolves through primitives and modules at the resumer\n   840\texactly as it did at the emitter, because there is no third store to\n   841\tintervene; a slice key cannot collide with a receiver task's key because no\n   842\treceiver key participates; resource entries are remapped to fresh local\n   843\tkeys (cursor cells per §7.5.3, stream handles per §7.4.3), and non-resource\n   844\tentries keep their keys verbatim. \"Unreachable store entries do not travel\"\n   845\tsurvives unchanged — anything the program did not observe is not the\n   846\tprogram's state — and nothing else shares state with the resumed task by\n   847\taccident.\n   848\t\n   849\t### 7.6.3 The migration unit is one task\n   850\t\n  1180\t   ready-queue is empty, else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n  1181\t2. **Enter exporting** (§7.7.4): detach the wait set into the export\n  1182\t   record, neutralize direct `:resume`. From here the source is fenced; no\n  1183\t   poll, retry, or local resume can perform an effect.\n  1184\t3. `lift(export-record)`: confirm the record is at a safepoint (§7.4.1),\n  1185\t   else `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; canonicalize every referenced segment and\n  1186\t   compute its address (§7.3.2), else the refusal; encode (§7.5), else the\n  1187\t   refusal; compute `:yin.k/requires` and `:yin.k/store` by the fixed point\n  1188\t   (§7.6), recording the discovery status; stamp the contract (§7.3.3); on\n  1189\t   first export, mint the occurrence; mint `:yin.k/id`.\n  1190\t4. Append the canonical instruction vectors of every required segment to a\n  1191\t   `dao.jing` intake pool (the payloads *are* the vectors, §7.3.4), then\n  1192\t   the id-less value body to the carrier medium; observe `:dao.stream/ok`\n  1193\t   on each. `:yin.k/carried` may then be empty. Retry of any failed append\n  1194\t   reuses the occurrence (§7.7.4).\n  1195\t5. Under `:exclusive`, append `:yin.k/offered` to the arbitration medium.\n  1196\t   The export record stays for recovery; the emitter is now a candidate\n  1197\t   like any other, and wakes only on custody (a grant to itself), never on\n  1198\t   data.\n  1199\t\n  1200\t**Lower** (resumer side):\n  1201\t\n  1202\t1. Read the value; check `:yin.k/contract`, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\n  1203\t2. Verify `:yin.k/id` by re-hashing the body, else `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`.\n  1204\t3. Decode against the §7.5.1 grammar with full structural validation, else\n  1205\t   `:yin.k/undecodable` — a correct hash is not structural validation, and\n  1206\t   both checks always run.\n  1207\t4. Evaluate `:yin.k/requires` (§7.6.5); fetch what may be fetched (segments\n  1208\t   by address, verified by re-canonicalization); else\n  1209\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with the discovery status.\n  1210\t5. Under `:exclusive`, attach to the arbitration medium (an attachment\n  1211\t   failure here is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming it — the value declares its\n  1212\t   authority, §7.7.3), propose, then observe the grant, else\n  1213\t   `:yin.k/not-holder` with the lease state observed; while a proposal is\n  1214\t   unanswered the driver's step answers `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` — a\n  1215\t   grantor owes no answer and no bound applies (`dao.lease.md`,\n  1216\t   *Authority*).\n  1217\t6. Load every required segment by address into the per-stamp index,\n  1218\t   verifying each (§7.3.4).\n  1219\t7. **Restore into an isolated store** (§7.6.2): the slice, the remapped\n  1220\t   cells, the resumer's FFI pair. Attach to every stream descriptor in the\n  1221\t   frame, pending waits, and cells (§7.4.3); an attachment `not-found` is\n  1222\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream. **Any failure from this step\n  1223\t   onward — after custody was acquired — releases first**: the resumer\n  1224\t   appends `:dao.lease/released`, the occurrence returns to offered, and\n  1225\t   the failure outcome is returned. A resumer that dies without releasing\n  1226\t   is recovered by the lease's own lapse; nothing else is owed.\n  1227\t8. `lower(frame, layout)` into engine registers; re-mint every pending wait\n  1228\t   in the local wait set; seed `:id-counter` per §7.6.3; run — through a\n  1229\t   fenced writer under `:exclusive` (§7.7.5).\n  1230\t9. At the next safepoint or halt, produce the successor occurrence\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
