{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1208,1315p;1840,1888p;2055,2088p;1890,1925p'\"","aggregated_output":"  1208\t     and alias in the other's. Every waiter is restored with `'foo`\n  1209\t     as its value after its own attach and lower; so is every later\n  1210\t     requirer.\n  1211\t\n  1212\t  **The closure marker, and its lift and lower per binding discipline.**\n  1213\t  UCF section 7.5.1 defines the closure marker for the named semantic\n  1214\t  VM. The de Bruijn kernels are positional and nameless, so the marker\n  1215\t  gains a `:yin.k/binding` key with two variants, and a module's\n  1216\t  closures carry the `:yin.k/store-of` key of the module store; both\n  1217\t  are amendments to UCF listed in section 11, item 12:\n  1218\t\n  1219\t  ```clojure\n  1220\t  ;; named (:yin.ast/code, :yin.semantic/code)\n  1221\t  {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/closure :yin.k/binding :named\n  1222\t   :yin.k/segment addr :yin.k/entry pc\n  1223\t   :yin.k/params [sym ...] :yin.k/env {sym encoded}\n  1224\t   :yin.k/store-of m}                       ; when module-defined\n  1225\t\n  1226\t  ;; positional (:yin.debruijn.code, :yin.debruijn.register)\n  1227\t  {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/closure :yin.k/binding :positional\n  1228\t   :yin.k/segment identity :yin.k/entry pc\n  1229\t   :yin.k/arity n :yin.k/frames [[encoded ...] ...]\n  1230\t   :yin.k/store-of m}                       ; when module-defined\n  1231\t  ```\n  1232\t\n  1233\t  | Kernel closure                       | Lift                          |\n  1234\t  |--------------------------------------|-------------------------------|\n  1235\t  | semantic `{:type :closure :params p  | `addr` = alias column inverse |\n  1236\t  | :entry e :segment local :env E}`     | of `local`; `pc` = `e`;       |\n  1237\t  |                                      | `params` = `p`; `env` =       |\n  1238\t  |                                      | `encode(E)`                   |\n  1239\t  | walker `{:type :closure :params p    | `addr` = the closure's        |\n  1240\t  | :body node :lambda id :env E}` (the  | recorded `:lambda` row id,    |\n  1241\t  | body is an AST node, not a code      | verified against `node` and   |\n  1242\t  | coordinate)                          | `p` by the rule below;        |\n  1243\t  |                                      | `pc` = `nil`;                 |\n  1244\t  |                                      | `params` = `p`; `env` =       |\n  1245\t  |                                      | `encode(E)`                   |\n  1246\t  | stack / register `{:type :closure    | offset-table entry with       |\n  1247\t  | :arity n :body-pc p :frames F}`      | `off <= p < off + len` gives  |\n  1248\t  |                                      | `identity` and `pc = p - off`;|\n  1249\t  |                                      | `arity` = `n`; `frames` =     |\n  1250\t  |                                      | `mapv (mapv encode) F`,       |\n  1251\t  |                                      | outermost frame first         |\n  1252\t\n  1253\t  | Marker variant | Lower into the parent                              |\n  1254\t  |----------------|----------------------------------------------------|\n  1255\t  | `:named`,      | `local'` = parent alias column at `addr`; `:entry` |\n  1256\t  | semantic       | unchanged; `:params` unchanged; `:env` =           |\n  1257\t  |                | `decode(env)`                                      |\n  1258\t  | `:named`,      | `:body` = the body slot of the parent's row at     |\n  1259\t  | walker         | `addr`, reconstructed as a node by the walker's    |\n  1260\t  |                | row decoder; `:params` unchanged; `:env` =         |\n  1261\t  |                | `decode(env)`                                      |\n  1262\t  | `:positional`  | `off'` = parent offset-table entry for `identity`; |\n  1263\t  |                | `:body-pc` = `off' + pc`; `:arity` = `n`;          |\n  1264\t  |                | `:frames` = `mapv (mapv decode) frames`            |\n  1265\t\n  1266\t  **The walker rule, exactly.** A walker closure holds its body as a\n  1267\t  live AST node, so its code coordinate is not a field of the closure\n  1268\t  but a fact about the rows: the body is the `body` slot of a\n  1269\t  `:lambda` row in the rows the child holds, and that row has an id\n  1270\t  (its `segment-key`, `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section 4.1). The\n  1271\t  row is **not** found by the node alone: rows are content-addressed\n  1272\t  and shared, and `semantic-bytecode->ast` rebuilds \"a row reached\n  1273\t  through several parents ... once and shared\", so one body node can\n  1274\t  sit under several `:lambda` rows with different params, and a\n  1275\t  `node -> row id` index cannot select among them. The id therefore\n  1276\t  crosses in two stages, because two different machines build the\n  1277\t  two values: the row decoder builds AST nodes at load, while a\n  1278\t  runtime closure is instantiated only when the `:lambda` node is\n  1279\t  evaluated. At load, the decoder annotates each decoded `:lambda`\n  1280\t  node with its source row id -- or files it in a side index\n  1281\t  `lambda-node -> row id`; the rebuild memoizes one node per row\n  1282\t  id, so the mapping is unambiguous -- and at evaluation the\n  1283\t  runtime `:lambda` transition (`ast-walker`'s `case :lambda`)\n  1284\t  copies the annotation into the closure it instantiates. The lift\n  1285\t  reads the recorded id and verifies the row's body slot is the\n  1286\t  closure's node and its params are the closure's params; a\n  1287\t  disagreement is the same `:yin.k/non-portable` `:unrooted-body`\n  1288\t  refusal as an id in no held row. A closure with no recorded id,\n  1289\t  which only a host function can fabricate, resolves through the\n  1290\t  walker's row index keyed by the pair `[node params]` (the index\n  1291\t  `vm-load-rows` builds when it decodes rows into nodes and\n  1292\t  `attach-image` extends), and refuses when the pair matches no\n  1293\t  row. The origin image of a walker closure\n  1294\t  is the tree whose rows contain that `:lambda` row; the `:lambda`\n  1295\t  row id is content-addressed, so the same lambda shared by two\n  1296\t  trees is one id and either tree serves as origin. The lower reads\n  1297\t  the parent's row at that id (present after act 3) and decodes its\n  1298\t  body slot into a node through the same decoder the loader uses, so\n  1299\t  the closure's `:body` in the parent is structurally equal to the\n  1300\t  child's and its `:params` and decoded `:env` are carried unchanged.\n  1301\t\n  1302\t  A `:named` marker lowers only into a named kernel and a `:positional`\n  1303\t  marker only into a positional kernel of the same `:format`; the\n  1304\t  reverse is `:binding-mismatch`. This never arises within one install,\n  1305\t  because the child is a VM of the same backend as the parent (section\n  1306\t  7.3, first rule); it is stated so that a lifted slice carried\n  1307\t  elsewhere fails closed rather than being reinterpreted. Converting a\n  1308\t  closure between disciplines is a re-lowering of source under a\n  1309\t  derivation record (section 8.1), never a relocation. `decode` and\n  1310\t  `encode` recurse through frames and environments, so a captured\n  1311\t  closure is relocated by the same rules and its origin joins the\n  1312\t  origin set of act 2.\n  1313\t\n  1314\t  **The module store (r6, amended r7).** A child runs with an\n  1315\t  isolated store, and its exports must not leak that isolation. A\n  1840\tscheduler with the section 7.3 phases; `link-module`; the UCF table\n  1841\tamendments. Tests, per backend: a require of an unlinked module over\n  1842\tring buffers completes and the program resumes with its exports bound;\n  1843\ta manifest declaring a different name is `:module-name-mismatch`; a\n  1844\tmanifest whose semantic, H, or R derivation leads from a different tree\n  1845\tis `:derivation-mismatch` (three swapped-image tests, and a fourth\n  1846\twhere the `:yin.ast/code` tree is not the manifest's tree); a\n  1847\tsame-named primitive of different profile at the receiver is\n  1848\t`:unresolved-free`; a shadowed export name in the requiring program's\n  1849\tstore is `:shadowed-free`; a free name the manifest never declares is\n  1850\t`:undeclared-free`; two outstanding requires on one response stream\n  1851\teach restore on their own id; a response landing before the entry would\n  1852\thave been polled is not skipped; a module requiring another links\n  1853\ttransitively while a third task keeps running; a require cycle is\n  1854\t`:require-cycle`; a module that binds a name and exports a closure\n  1855\treading it links, the closure reads the module's value in the\n  1856\tparent, a parent binding of the same name neither supplies nor\n  1857\tshadows it, and a write from one export is visible to the next\n  1858\tapplication in the same task and to no other task; a read inside a\n  1859\tbody applied before its definition retains its obligation and is\n  1860\t`:undeclared-free` unless the manifest declares it; an export-only\n  1861\timage with no application site produces no obligations; a parked\n  1862\tentry recorded before an attach restores after it at the same pcs\n  1863\twith `:segment` never assigned from the entry; a register call in\n  1864\tflight while a nested require attaches an image returns into the\n  1865\tgrown code space, the attachment intact after the return; two tasks\n  1866\tholding images of different lengths require the same module and each\n  1867\tapplies its export correctly; a `:store-put` executed inside an\n  1868\texported closure writes the module store, and a module closure\n  1869\tcalling another module's closure writes each module's own store; a\n  1870\tslice re-exporting a dependency closure carries the dependency's\n  1871\tstore snapshot with the child's mutations, and a task already\n  1872\tholding that dependency's store keeps its own instance; a module\n  1873\tforging a predictable resource key (`[:store-get :stream-0]`)\n  1874\tagainst a task with a live stream reads nothing on any backend;\n  1875\tREPL input following a module closure's tail-completed application\n  1876\tdefines into the task's own store, no stale module context; a\n  1877\twalker closure whose body node is shared by two `:lambda` rows\n  1878\twith different params lifts from its recorded source row and\n  1879\trefuses `:unrooted-body` when a fabricated `[node params]` pair\n  1880\tmatches no row; `(vm :stack)` and `(vm :register)` in `yin.repl` link\n  1881\tthe same manifest by H and R respectively and produce B0-equal results.\n  1882\t\n  1883\t**M5. `yin.repl` wiring.** The REPL composes the link pair per VM and\n  1884\tthe content pair per connection (`connect` already opens the RPC\n  1885\tclient). `(require 'foo)` at the prompt exercises the whole path.\n  1886\t\n  1887\tCoexistence during M4: host modules are entered by\n  1888\t`register-host-module` from the first commit of M4, so every existing\n  1890\tperiod in which two registry shapes coexist.\n  1891\t\n  1892\t## 10. File box\n  1893\t\n  1894\t```text\n  1895\tRenamed:  src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker.cljc\n  1896\t            -> src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc            (M1)\n  1897\t          test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_test.cljc\n  1898\t            -> test/yin/vm/linker_test.cljc           (M1)\n  1899\tNew:      test/yin/vm/linker_step_test.cljc           (M3)\n  1900\t          test/yin/vm/linker_authority_test.cljc      (M4 entry)\n  1901\t          test/yin/vm/linker_require_test.cljc        (M4)\n  1902\tEdited:   src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc                 (M4)\n  1903\t          src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc  (:link-request, :link-response,\n  1904\t                                        :install wait reasons; the\n  1905\t                                        install child; module-store\n  1906\t                                        resolution and routing; the\n  1907\t                                        private :resources table)   (M4)\n  1908\t          src/cljc/yin/vm/content.cljc (retire load-rows,\n  1909\t                                        fetch-vector)              (M2)\n  1910\t          src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc, semantic.cljc,\n  1911\t          debruijn/stack.cljc, debruijn/register.cljc\n  1912\t            (attach-image; offset table and image\n  1913\t             identities on the positional kernels; row index\n  1914\t             on the walker; closures carry :store-of; the\n  1915\t             register frame drops :segment and :hash; store\n  1916\t             routing and its frame threading)                      (M4)\n  1917\t          src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc  (append-*-image over attach-image;\n  1918\t                                   the four streams)          (M4, M5)\n  1919\t          docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\n  1920\t            (section 7.4.1 rows, 7.4.3 variants, 7.5.1 marker\n  1921\t             keys :yin.k/binding and :yin.k/store-of)              (M4)\n  1922\tDepends:  dao.jing (segment-key, segment-matches?, materialize!)\n  1923\t          dao.jing.remote, dao.jing.remote.step, dao.stream.rpc\n  1924\t          yin.vm (validate-rows, free-names, primitive-profiles)\n  1925\t          yin.vm.code (well-formed-vector?)\n  2055\t    reads the module's `x`, while a parent binding of the same name\n  2056\t    neither supplies nor shadows it; a write the closure makes to\n  2057\t    `x` through a direct `:store-put` instruction is visible to a\n  2058\t    sibling export applied afterwards in the same task and to no\n  2059\t    other task's module store; the same module linked by two tasks\n  2060\t    holds two isolated stores; a slice whose exports reach a\n  2061\t    dependency's store transitively carries that dependency's\n  2062\t    snapshot, including a mutation the child made through the\n  2063\t    dependency's closure, a missing snapshot is\n  2064\t    `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :missing-module-store`,\n  2065\t    and a receiving task that already holds the dependency's store\n  2066\t    keeps its own instance; a module closure calling another\n  2067\t    module's closure routes each body's reads and writes to its own\n  2068\t    module; in the child, a closure re-exported from a dependency\n  2069\t    reads that dependency's module store, not the child's ambient\n  2070\t    bindings; a module emitting `[:store-get :stream-0]` against a\n  2071\t    task with a live stream at that id reads nothing on any\n  2072\t    backend, because engine resources live in the private table no\n  2073\t    store instruction reads; after a module closure applied at the\n  2074\t    REPL and its tail call completed, new top-level input defining\n  2075\t    a name of its own binds the task's store, never the module's;\n  2076\t    a walker closure whose body node sits under two `:lambda` rows\n  2077\t    with different params lifts from its recorded source row --\n  2078\t    annotated on the decoded node at load, copied into the closure\n  2079\t    at evaluation -- and a fabricated closure with no recorded row\n  2080\t    resolves by the `[node params]` index or refuses\n  2081\t    `:unrooted-body`.\n  2082\t\n  2083\t## 12. Open decisions\n  2084\t\n  2085\tRecorded so a later plan does not rediscover them. None blocks M1 to M3;\n  2086\tthe first concerns material the composition supplies to a policy section\n  2087\t8.2 fixes.\n  2088\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
