{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '180,245p;775,810p'\"","aggregated_output":"   180\t …]\n   181\t```\n   182\t\n   183\tRules, each replacing heavier machinery from the attributed-map record:\n   184\t\n   185\t- **pc is the index.** No entity ids and no attribute names appear anywhere\n   186\t  in the form: position fixes meaning, the §2.4 table fixes shape, and a\n   187\t  query over code is a query over tuples of the vector, not over EAV.\n   188\t- **Saturation.** Defaults the loader applies are materialized in the tuple —\n   189\t  `:gensym` absent prefix becomes `\"id\"`, `:ffi-call` absent argc becomes\n   190\t  `0`, `:stream-make` absent buffer becomes the default capacity — so a batch\n   191\t  that omitted a defaulted operand and one that stated it canonicalize\n   192\t  identically, because they execute identically.\n   193\t- **Refs are resolved pcs**, exactly as the loader resolves them.\n   194\t- **The header folds away.** `:yin.code/length` is `(count vector)`,\n   195\t  `:yin.code/type :segment` is implied by the form. Provenance\n   196\t  (`:yin.code/derived-from`, every `:yin.code/source`), entity ids, batch\n   197\t  order, `t`, `m`, and `:yin.code/hash` itself are excluded — the address is\n   198\t  computed *over* the vector, which therefore cannot contain it without\n   199\t  hashing the hash being verified.\n   200\t- **Admissible slots.** Each tuple is its mnemonic plus exactly the operands\n   201\t  the §2.4 table gives it, in the table's order. A batch that resolves to\n   202\t  anything else — a mnemonic outside the table, an operand the mnemonic's\n   203\t  tuple has no slot for, a segment-entity attribute beyond the folded and\n   204\t  excluded set — is **non-canonicalizable**, and canonicalization refuses\n   205\t  naming the instruction (the lifted value is never minted; the refusal is\n   206\t  `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :non-canonicalizable`).\n   207\t- **Ordering: none is imposed.** The vector's order is its positions;\n   208\t  canonicalization sorts nothing and compares no operand against another.\n   209\t  Literal operands are values and hash under the same encoder as everything\n   210\t  else, so equal literals address equally however their maps were built.\n   211\t  The first draft's `[:segment …]`-versus-`[0 …]` triple sort — which throws\n   212\t  `Keyword cannot be cast to Number` in the very host it must run on — has\n   213\t  no successor here because there is no sort at all.\n   214\t\n   215\t**The address** is `(dao.jing/segment-key vector)` — a\n   216\t`:segment/sha256-<hex>` keyword. The vector *is* the resolved\n   217\tinterpretation, so r2's collision-freedom argument carries over unchanged\n   218\tand needs only restating: canonicalization and execution read the same\n   219\tvalue by construction; there is nothing left to collide. The address fixes\n   220\tthe resolved instruction stream — mnemonics, operands, control graph,\n   221\tsaturations — and nothing else.\n   222\t\n   223\t**What equal addresses do and do not mean.** Two segments address equally\n   224\t**iff** their loader-resolved interpretations are equal; this includes\n   225\tsegments that differ only in entity ids, batch order, provenance, or omitted\n   226\tdefaulted operands. No equivalence beyond that is claimed: different pc\n   227\tlayouts, different `:gensym` prefixes, different but *equivalent* instruction\n   228\tsequences, and compiler-renamed artifacts address **differently**. The\n   229\taddress is a fingerprint of one instruction stream, not a quotient of a\n   230\tsemantics.\n   231\t\n   232\tThe canonical encoding beneath `segment-key` is transitional\n   233\t(`dao.jing.md`, *Open items*). UCF inherits that limit unchanged: addresses\n   234\tare portable exactly as far as `dao.jing` addresses are, and when the pinned\n   235\tbyte encoding lands, `:yin.code/hash` values change with every other minted\n   236\taddress. This is the correct dependency; UCF must not mint a second\n   237\taddressing scheme.\n   238\t\n   239\t### 7.3.3 The contract stamp\n   240\t\n   241\tAn address fixes *which* instructions; it does not fix what `:call` *means*.\n   242\tInterpretation is supplied by the loader and the step loop (§1.1,\n   243\t*Interpretation Creates Semantics*), and the first draft pinned only the\n   244\topcode table — leaving resolution precedence, effect outcomes, call\n   245\trestoration, and scheduling outside the stamp. UCF therefore versions the\n   775\t  a name. A captured environment contributes reachable values, never name\n   776\t  discharge.\n   777\t- **Code:** every reachable segment's instructions are walked — as datoms\n   778\t  where the emitter holds the batch, as canonical tuples where only a vector\n   779\t  was fetched (§7.3.4) — including **`:store-get` and `:store-put`\n   780\t  operands**, which name store\n   781\t  keys directly and which the first draft missed entirely; every `:var`\n   782\t  name; every effect op (`:stream-*`, `:ffi-call`, and the effects of\n   783\t  `:effectful`-class primitives the walk finds); every `:resume` operand\n   784\t  (a parked id, §7.6.3). Discovering a new work item continues the fixed point\n   785\t  with that address and context even when the address was already walked for\n   786\t  another context.\n   787\t- **Names:** only a free `:var` name is an obligation. An obligation is\n   788\t  discharged by a matching key in the reachable store slice, or retained as\n   789\t  a required primitive or module export and checked by profile (§7.5.2). An\n   790\t  environment never discharges a name: captured environments contribute\n   791\t  values to their work item's reachability analysis, while lexical binding\n   792\t  determines whether a `:var` is free before it becomes an obligation. If no\n   793\t  store key or profiled primitive/module requirement can discharge an\n   794\t  obligation, discovery is `:incomplete`; after lowering, an unavailable\n   795\t  retained requirement is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` (§7.6.5).\n   796\t- **Modules:** a module's manifest declares its exported primitive profiles\n   797\t  and its **store footprint** (store keys and effect kinds its handlers may\n   798\t  touch). Until manifests carry footprints, any required module whose\n   799\t  footprint is undeclared sets `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete` — the honest\n   800\t  statement that closure could not be computed, never a silent\n   801\t  under-approximation. A module effect handler may read store keys the\n   802\t  static walk cannot see; a composition may supply extra slice keys\n   803\t  explicitly, and the value records that it did.\n   804\t\n   805\tBecause the computation is over data, the same query answers \"what does this\n   806\tprogram need?\" for a segment that has never run. And because discovery can\n   807\t*fail to terminate successfully* — a missing segment cannot be walked, an\n   808\tundeclared footprint cannot be closed — the result distinguishes\n   809\t`:complete` from `:incomplete` from `:blocked` (§7.6.5); a fixed point that\n   810\tcould not finish is reported as such, not passed off as a satisfied closure.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
